1
00:00:00,048 --> 00:00:02,308
So that's the base config then. But then

2
00:00:02,436 --> 00:00:05,113
below that, I got a bunch of functions where

3
00:00:05,225 --> 00:00:08,704
I actually get data, manipulate data, and

4
00:00:08,832 --> 00:00:11,541
even have a transaction because SQLite has

5
00:00:11,573 --> 00:00:15,068
support for transactions where the idea is

6
00:00:15,180 --> 00:00:18,194
that multiple SQL commands, multiple SQL

7
00:00:18,242 --> 00:00:21,817
statements can be executed together or none

8
00:00:21,817 --> 00:00:24,638
at all so that if one of multiple statements

9
00:00:24,638 --> 00:00:27,411
fails, the others are rolled back as well,

10
00:00:27,828 --> 00:00:29,784
which is of course great for deleting stuff

11
00:00:29,784 --> 00:00:32,204
if you have to touch and adjust multiple

12
00:00:32,316 --> 00:00:33,567
entries across multiple

13
00:00:34,257 --> 00:00:36,261
tables, which is not the case in this

14
00:00:36,261 --> 00:00:38,025
example, but which could be the case in more

15
00:00:38,025 --> 00:00:40,751
complex applications, then you might want to

16
00:00:40,751 --> 00:00:42,194
run these different statements in a

17
00:00:42,194 --> 00:00:44,535
transaction so that if one of them fails,

18
00:00:44,984 --> 00:00:47,903
all of them fail and are rolled back. That's

19
00:00:47,967 --> 00:00:50,468
the idea behind transactions. And Bun's

20
00:00:50,468 --> 00:00:53,499
SQLite client has support for transactions.

21
00:00:53,964 --> 00:00:55,712
You just create a transaction by using

22
00:00:55,824 --> 00:00:58,278
db.transaction, the transaction method

23
00:00:58,871 --> 00:01:01,581
that then takes a callback function. In that

24
00:01:01,581 --> 00:01:03,329
callback function, you have your normal

25
00:01:03,746 --> 00:01:06,826
query code, your normal DB query command.

26
00:01:07,435 --> 00:01:09,585
And then whenever this transaction function,

27
00:01:09,778 --> 00:01:11,895
delete note transaction in my case here,

28
00:01:12,264 --> 00:01:15,168
whenever that is executed, that is when the

29
00:01:15,168 --> 00:01:17,205
statements inside of it are actually

30
00:01:17,237 --> 00:01:18,826
executed. That's the idea behind a

31
00:01:18,826 --> 00:01:21,232
transaction. So even that support is built

32
00:01:21,248 --> 00:01:21,409
in.

33
00:01:21,970 --> 00:01:24,344
But let's take a step back. How does query

34
00:01:24,601 --> 00:01:25,050
work then?

35
00:01:25,724 --> 00:01:28,307
We can already see here that I'm using query

36
00:01:28,387 --> 00:01:28,820
here to

37
00:01:29,350 --> 00:01:32,016
delete from notes. So query is not just for

38
00:01:32,064 --> 00:01:33,799
fetching data, but as mentioned before,

39
00:01:33,992 --> 00:01:37,125
whenever you have a command that tends to be

40
00:01:37,141 --> 00:01:39,439
executed more often, because then some

41
00:01:39,551 --> 00:01:41,881
internal optimizations kick in and the

42
00:01:41,881 --> 00:01:43,022
command gets cached.

43
00:01:43,568 --> 00:01:45,705
And what we see is that here in this query,

44
00:01:46,139 --> 00:01:48,581
and also in many other queries down there,

45
00:01:49,352 --> 00:01:50,444
we have some

46
00:01:51,184 --> 00:01:54,397
dynamic parts in that query. For example,

47
00:01:54,413 --> 00:01:57,786
here for creating a note, I'm using query to

48
00:01:57,930 --> 00:02:00,371
insert something into notes. I wanna insert

49
00:02:00,371 --> 00:02:02,602
the title and the content of a note. And the

50
00:02:02,602 --> 00:02:05,781
concrete values are of course unknown in

51
00:02:05,877 --> 00:02:06,487
advance

52
00:02:07,017 --> 00:02:09,008
because they depend on whatever the user

53
00:02:09,104 --> 00:02:10,148
entered into the form.

54
00:02:10,934 --> 00:02:14,161
And in Bun, when using Bun's SQLite client,

55
00:02:14,386 --> 00:02:16,377
you have different ways of defining such

56
00:02:16,553 --> 00:02:19,298
placeholders in your queries in a safe way.

57
00:02:20,021 --> 00:02:22,076
For example, using this dollar sign

58
00:02:22,413 --> 00:02:23,023
notation.

59
00:02:24,193 --> 00:02:26,341
So here are the placeholder names, the

60
00:02:26,357 --> 00:02:28,473
variables, whatever you wanna call them.

61
00:02:28,922 --> 00:02:31,021
They have any name of your choice, could be

62
00:02:31,149 --> 00:02:33,281
title, could be t, that is up to you,

63
00:02:33,810 --> 00:02:36,182
but they are prefixed with a dollar sign.

64
00:02:36,679 --> 00:02:39,340
And alternatively, Bun would also allow for

65
00:02:39,564 --> 00:02:42,914
an@ symbol, for example, or a colon. You can

66
00:02:43,010 --> 00:02:45,190
use any of those. I typically use the dollar

67
00:02:45,190 --> 00:02:48,428
sign. Alternatively, you could also use the

68
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:50,159
order and then use

69
00:02:51,185 --> 00:02:51,329
?1,

70
00:02:52,146 --> 00:02:53,140
question mark 2

71
00:02:53,685 --> 00:02:55,753
to make it clear that the first argument

72
00:02:55,753 --> 00:02:59,119
passed to get should be inserted here, the

73
00:02:59,119 --> 00:03:01,075
second argument should be inserted here.

74
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:03,880
But I like to have named arguments. But

75
00:03:04,570 --> 00:03:06,798
that takes us to the next question. How do

76
00:03:06,814 --> 00:03:08,914
we pass in the concrete values that should

77
00:03:08,914 --> 00:03:11,302
be inserted here? Well, I just mentioned it,

78
00:03:11,318 --> 00:03:14,076
you pass them to the function you call after

79
00:03:14,268 --> 00:03:17,410
query. So query, the query method is used to

80
00:03:17,410 --> 00:03:19,878
define a query, but it doesn't execute it

81
00:03:19,878 --> 00:03:22,828
yet with SQLite. Instead, if you just have

82
00:03:22,860 --> 00:03:25,204
this code here, The query is defined, it's

83
00:03:25,413 --> 00:03:27,663
optimized and cached, but it's not executed

84
00:03:27,663 --> 00:03:27,920
yet.

85
00:03:28,466 --> 00:03:31,262
It gets executed when you then call get, for

86
00:03:31,375 --> 00:03:33,913
example. And I'm saying for example because

87
00:03:33,946 --> 00:03:36,468
there are other methods as well, like all.

88
00:03:36,854 --> 00:03:38,798
If you had a SQL command that would return

89
00:03:38,847 --> 00:03:41,193
multiple rows, all would give you access to

90
00:03:41,321 --> 00:03:41,627
all of them.

91
00:03:42,205 --> 00:03:44,776
Get would be used if you just wanna get back

92
00:03:44,937 --> 00:03:47,026
one result. And for example, the insert

93
00:03:47,026 --> 00:03:49,211
statement just returns one row.

94
00:03:49,917 --> 00:03:50,319
Result.

95
00:03:50,961 --> 00:03:53,353
It gives you back the ID of the entry that

96
00:03:53,369 --> 00:03:55,199
was inserted because of that returning

97
00:03:55,231 --> 00:03:55,520
clause.

98
00:03:56,435 --> 00:03:59,807
So it's that method, getAll, whatever you

99
00:03:59,871 --> 00:04:02,793
use, that you chain on the query that

100
00:04:02,921 --> 00:04:05,394
actually executes the query and that

101
00:04:05,426 --> 00:04:07,834
therefore needs to provide the input for the

102
00:04:07,834 --> 00:04:10,017
query, so the concrete values for these

103
00:04:10,081 --> 00:04:12,570
placeholders. And in this example here where

104
00:04:12,570 --> 00:04:15,267
we create a note, I'm passing an input

105
00:04:15,443 --> 00:04:17,224
object to createNote.

106
00:04:17,865 --> 00:04:20,494
That input object, if we take a look, is of

107
00:04:20,494 --> 00:04:23,491
type NoteInput, which is just an object with

108
00:04:23,556 --> 00:04:25,688
a title and a content where both are string.

109
00:04:26,136 --> 00:04:28,509
So such a JavaScript object with title and

110
00:04:28,589 --> 00:04:32,981
content is passed to createNote. And I have

111
00:04:33,013 --> 00:04:35,899
to use title and content as names in my

112
00:04:35,899 --> 00:04:38,816
NoteInput type and object because I'm using

113
00:04:38,864 --> 00:04:41,109
title and content as names here. So the

114
00:04:41,109 --> 00:04:44,251
names have to match. If I named this here T,

115
00:04:45,036 --> 00:04:47,825
then I should expect an input where I have a

116
00:04:47,921 --> 00:04:48,034
T

117
00:04:49,525 --> 00:04:50,103
property,

118
00:04:51,450 --> 00:04:53,375
which I don't in this app. So I'll go back

119
00:04:53,375 --> 00:04:55,957
to title. That is really important

120
00:04:56,486 --> 00:04:57,224
also down here.

121
00:04:58,186 --> 00:05:02,244
And that is then a SQL injection safe way of

122
00:05:02,822 --> 00:05:05,212
inserting values into a query. So that is

123
00:05:05,228 --> 00:05:06,479
how you should do it.

124
00:05:07,008 --> 00:05:09,045
And I'm doing that in all those places also

125
00:05:09,077 --> 00:05:11,852
for getting a note. For example, here we see

126
00:05:12,221 --> 00:05:15,027
that I have get and I pass in an object

127
00:05:15,027 --> 00:05:17,578
where I map ID to ID. Just using the

128
00:05:17,578 --> 00:05:19,874
shortcut for it. And I'm using ID as a name

129
00:05:19,922 --> 00:05:22,410
because I'm using ID as a name here. If I

130
00:05:22,474 --> 00:05:26,631
were using i here, then I would map my ID to

131
00:05:27,290 --> 00:05:28,140
a value of i,

132
00:05:28,670 --> 00:05:29,906
to a property named i.

133
00:05:30,436 --> 00:05:32,169
I also need to adjust the type here then

134
00:05:33,164 --> 00:05:34,465
to make TypeScript happy.

135
00:05:35,075 --> 00:05:37,145
And so that's how passing values into

136
00:05:37,177 --> 00:05:38,028
queries works.

137
00:05:38,718 --> 00:05:41,094
Now, you might remember that I mentioned

138
00:05:41,174 --> 00:05:43,165
earlier that we can configure our database

139
00:05:43,261 --> 00:05:44,208
when we create it like this.

140
00:05:44,786 --> 00:05:46,937
And I mentioned this strict setting to which

141
00:05:46,969 --> 00:05:47,739
I would come back to.

142
00:05:48,279 --> 00:05:51,518
Strict is related to those parameters, to

143
00:05:51,518 --> 00:05:53,801
these dynamic values you pass into your

144
00:05:53,947 --> 00:05:54,255
queries.

145
00:05:55,172 --> 00:05:57,551
If you set strict to false,

146
00:05:59,094 --> 00:05:59,319
then

147
00:05:59,898 --> 00:06:01,859
you actually have to

148
00:06:02,872 --> 00:06:04,688
use the dollar signs here

149
00:06:05,604 --> 00:06:07,693
in your property names as well.

150
00:06:08,368 --> 00:06:10,376
So then this would be the correct way of

151
00:06:10,504 --> 00:06:12,095
passing values into those queries.

152
00:06:12,674 --> 00:06:14,448
That's of course a bit annoying, which is

153
00:06:14,546 --> 00:06:16,475
why you typically wanna set strict to true

154
00:06:16,475 --> 00:06:17,734
so that you can use that simpler form.

155
00:06:19,736 --> 00:06:21,836
As weird as that might sound, that strict

156
00:06:21,836 --> 00:06:24,080
makes it simpler. But strict has another

157
00:06:24,080 --> 00:06:26,501
effect. With strict set to true,

158
00:06:27,110 --> 00:06:28,585
you'll get an error

159
00:06:29,130 --> 00:06:29,820
at runtime

160
00:06:30,397 --> 00:06:32,818
when you have an incorrect

161
00:06:33,619 --> 00:06:34,116
name here.

162
00:06:34,838 --> 00:06:36,137
So if I have i,

163
00:06:37,147 --> 00:06:39,503
map this to ID and I adjust this type, then

164
00:06:39,632 --> 00:06:41,507
TypeScript is happy, but this will fail

165
00:06:41,555 --> 00:06:44,105
because in the query I'm expecting ID, not

166
00:06:44,265 --> 00:06:44,297
i.

167
00:06:45,403 --> 00:06:47,552
At runtime, I would get an error with this

168
00:06:47,552 --> 00:06:50,664
code here without strict. It would just

169
00:06:50,857 --> 00:06:53,299
insert nothing as an ID. So it would not

170
00:06:53,492 --> 00:06:55,789
error, it would just insert nothing as an

171
00:06:55,821 --> 00:06:58,456
ID, which might cause an error because of

172
00:06:58,488 --> 00:07:01,026
constraints, but which could also fail for

173
00:07:01,138 --> 00:07:04,287
other SQL commands. So to get a clear error

174
00:07:04,432 --> 00:07:06,842
that tells us that we have a typo in our

175
00:07:06,842 --> 00:07:07,452
parameters,

176
00:07:08,030 --> 00:07:11,099
we should use strict mode. And that's what

177
00:07:11,163 --> 00:07:13,814
I'm doing. Now, when you're getting multiple

178
00:07:13,894 --> 00:07:16,545
entries, you can use all instead of get.

179
00:07:16,786 --> 00:07:19,193
Here without any parameter because I don't

180
00:07:19,274 --> 00:07:21,778
have any dynamic values in this command. But

181
00:07:21,778 --> 00:07:25,116
if you know that you'll get a lot of rows,

182
00:07:25,694 --> 00:07:28,519
you could also consider using iterate

183
00:07:28,984 --> 00:07:29,915
instead of all.

184
00:07:30,541 --> 00:07:32,917
So instead of all, you can use iterate and

185
00:07:32,933 --> 00:07:35,164
then you can loop through those rows to

186
00:07:35,260 --> 00:07:37,588
process the rows row by row instead of

187
00:07:37,588 --> 00:07:39,610
fetching them all at once, which

188
00:07:40,236 --> 00:07:41,889
would mean that they get all loaded into

189
00:07:41,921 --> 00:07:43,751
memory, which could be a problem if you're

190
00:07:43,848 --> 00:07:46,258
working on a lot of rows. Alternatively, Bun

191
00:07:46,468 --> 00:07:48,294
even allows you to loop directly on the

192
00:07:48,294 --> 00:07:50,960
query and it'll then execute it row by row

193
00:07:50,960 --> 00:07:53,077
behind the scenes. So that would be possible

194
00:07:53,077 --> 00:07:53,206
too

195
00:07:53,788 --> 00:07:56,038
if you're working with lots of rows.

196
00:07:56,637 --> 00:07:58,678
You don't need to do that if you just have a

197
00:07:58,808 --> 00:07:59,828
couple of dozen or

198
00:08:00,363 --> 00:08:01,303
a few hundred rows,

199
00:08:02,977 --> 00:08:04,969
depending on what's in those rows, of

200
00:08:04,985 --> 00:08:05,210
course.

201
00:08:06,110 --> 00:08:08,825
Now, one other thing you see here is this as

202
00:08:08,921 --> 00:08:11,556
method with as note. That's just a little

203
00:08:11,589 --> 00:08:14,058
convenience feature provided by Bun where

204
00:08:14,272 --> 00:08:15,195
you can tell it how it should convert

205
00:08:17,096 --> 00:08:19,934
that data when it retrieves it. So here with

206
00:08:20,143 --> 00:08:22,692
all, we'll get an array of all our notes.

207
00:08:23,029 --> 00:08:25,338
But to make sure that every single element

208
00:08:25,450 --> 00:08:29,106
in that array is of type Note and is using

209
00:08:29,203 --> 00:08:32,282
our class Note, I defined a class named

210
00:08:32,346 --> 00:08:34,815
Note. So not just a type, instead a real

211
00:08:34,831 --> 00:08:36,948
class, which is a real JavaScript feature

212
00:08:37,397 --> 00:08:39,498
with all the properties a note should have

213
00:08:39,498 --> 00:08:40,829
and a little utility method.

214
00:08:41,390 --> 00:08:43,458
And with this as method added,

215
00:08:44,292 --> 00:08:47,288
Bunn will create an instance of that class

216
00:08:47,400 --> 00:08:49,948
for every element, for every entry, every

217
00:08:50,124 --> 00:08:52,848
row it returned or it got out of the

218
00:08:52,864 --> 00:08:53,585
database here

219
00:08:54,130 --> 00:08:56,245
and will then therefore get back an array of

220
00:08:56,245 --> 00:08:58,952
such notes here. So getNotes returns an

221
00:08:58,952 --> 00:09:02,125
array of Note instances in my case. That is

222
00:09:02,221 --> 00:09:04,720
very useful. You have to create a class for

223
00:09:04,720 --> 00:09:07,380
that though. You can't just pass in a type

224
00:09:07,460 --> 00:09:09,944
or interface here because those don't exist

225
00:09:09,944 --> 00:09:12,539
at runtime. You need to pass in a class.

226
00:09:13,213 --> 00:09:14,800
And then last but not least, it's worth

227
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:17,541
noting that Bun also offers a close method

228
00:09:17,541 --> 00:09:19,480
on the database, which you don't necessarily

229
00:09:19,609 --> 00:09:21,484
have to call. It should do that

230
00:09:21,548 --> 00:09:23,712
automatically when the Bun process shuts

231
00:09:23,712 --> 00:09:25,989
down, but you can explicitly call it here.

232
00:09:26,518 --> 00:09:28,682
And I'm doing that in the end from my

233
00:09:28,762 --> 00:09:31,471
index.ts file with that cleanShutdown method

234
00:09:31,471 --> 00:09:33,363
I mentioned before, or that cleanShutdown

235
00:09:33,475 --> 00:09:34,709
function I mentioned before.

236
00:09:35,478 --> 00:09:38,123
And when you call close, that just does what

237
00:09:38,123 --> 00:09:40,368
the name implies. It cleanly closes the

238
00:09:40,368 --> 00:09:42,516
connection and does all the remaining

239
00:09:42,532 --> 00:09:44,455
cleanup work, like for example, this here,

240
00:09:44,953 --> 00:09:46,735
where it removes these redundant

241
00:09:47,506 --> 00:09:49,625
wall-related files. So if I start the

242
00:09:49,625 --> 00:09:52,515
server, those files show up. If I stop it,

243
00:09:52,579 --> 00:09:54,843
they are removed. And that happens because

244
00:09:54,843 --> 00:09:58,279
of these settings and because I call close

245
00:09:58,279 --> 00:09:58,696
in the end.

246
00:09:59,964 --> 00:10:03,818
Now, I won't dive into SQL syntax or

247
00:10:03,818 --> 00:10:05,728
anything like that because this is obviously

248
00:10:05,728 --> 00:10:06,740
not a SQL course,

249
00:10:07,270 --> 00:10:09,597
but this hopefully shows you how easy and

250
00:10:09,597 --> 00:10:11,011
powerful it is to

251
00:10:11,673 --> 00:10:14,818
interact with a SQLite database through

252
00:10:14,915 --> 00:10:18,609
Bun's built-in SQLite client. So it's yet

253
00:10:18,609 --> 00:10:20,642
another feature we can use in our apps

254
00:10:20,803 --> 00:10:23,352
without installing any extra dependencies.
